---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Architecture" description="amber architecture: a native C++17 agent runtime with a plugin/capability system, multi-provider support, MCP, sub-agents and terminal clients.">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Architecture</h1>

    <p>
      Every diagram on this page, and the rest of the system, is
      collected on the <a href="/architecture/diagrams">diagrams index</a>.
    </p>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        Amber is compiled C++17 from the runtime up. The agent loop, context
        management, tools, plugin system and clients share the same native
        core. The result is a small, explicit runtime with no Python or Node
        dependency, while the core remains usable as a library rather than
        being tied to a single interface.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="runtimeOverview"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Runtime</h2>
      <p>
        The runtime is the engine: the agent loop, context management, and
        the plugin system that installs capabilities. It has no UI and no
        <code>main()</code>. The three clients (<code>amber</code>,
        <code>amber-cli</code>, <code>amber-bench</code>) are thin shells
        over it. Embed the library in your own C++ project and you get the
        agent loop, tool registry, compression pipeline, plugin runtime and
        MCP transport without a terminal in sight.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Agent Loop</h2>
      <p>
        The agent loop sends the context to the LLM, receives tool calls,
        executes them through the tool registry, feeds results back, and
        repeats until the agent terminates. Streaming is via SSE; token
        deltas are surfaced through <code>AgentHooks</code> for live TUI
        rendering.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="agentLoopCycle"
      />
      <Diagram
        name="errorRecovery"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>
        The <code>Context</code> class is a pure stack, messages are sealed
        on <code>push()</code> and can never be modified in-place. Every
        push computes an FNV-1a hash chain
        (<code>h_i = FNV(prev_hash || msg)</code>); <code>get_all()</code>
        recomputes the entire chain and asserts integrity before returning.
        Any in-place mutation breaks a link and crashes in debug builds.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>h_0 = FNV(0              || msg_0)
h_1 = FNV(h_0            || msg_1)
h_2 = FNV(h_1            || msg_2)
...
chain_hash_ = h_n-1</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        The only mutation operations are <code>push</code> (append),
        <code>pop</code> (remove top, LIFO), <code>clear</code> (remove all),
        and <code>get_all</code> (read-only). There is no replace, insert, or
        update, if you need to rebuild, <code>clear()</code> then
        <code>push()</code> each message.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="contextHashChain"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Capability / Plugin System</h2>
      <p>
        The plugin system is the extensibility backbone. Plugins contribute
        tools, LLM providers, commands, prompt blocks, status segments,
        panels, settings, and log sinks, and observe the agent loop through
        typed events.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three mechanisms:
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Contribution registries</strong>: things that exist (tools, providers, status segments). A core plugin declares typed <code>Capability</code> objects; the runtime installs each into its registry and records it in a per-plugin ledger.</li>
        <li><strong>Typed events</strong>: things that happen. Payload structs in <code>events.h</code>; no <code>void*</code> in the plugin-facing API. Unsubscribed <code>publish()</code> is a single atomic load, no per-token events.</li>
        <li><strong>Host services</strong>: things the plugin needs the host to do. Registration never happens through events; UI state is pulled at render time, not pushed from worker threads.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <code>disable()</code> unwinds the ledger in reverse order, if a
        contribution can survive deactivation, the ledger is broken and that
        is a tested invariant. Every provider amber ships is a plugin, and
        the core declares none. With no plugins registered,
        <code>/provider list</code> is empty.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="pluginLifecycle"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Providers</h2>
      <p>
        Provider integrations live behind the same plugin architecture. A
        provider capability is flavor plus optional dialect factory plus
        presets; wire behavior lives in the <code>Dialect</code>. A provider
        plugin must not open its own HTTP client, it uses the transport's
        <code>openai</code> protocol, so a disabled provider plugin takes its
        protocol with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bundled provider plugins: Anthropic, Gemini, OpenRouter, DeepSeek,
        KiloCode, OpenCode, CommandCode, Custom, and OpenAI-compatible
        endpoints. Each owns its presets, protocol, and provider-specific
        features.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="llmDialectPath"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Tools</h2>
      <p>
        The built-in tool set (read, write, search, bash) arrives through
        the same capability path an extension would use, so the path cannot
        rot. Each tool has a name, description, parameter schema, and an
        <code>execute</code> method. The bash tool is approval-gated, each
        call must be approved by the host before it runs; if no approval
        handler is installed the call is denied (fail-safe).
      </p>
      <p>
        The search tool is pluggable: <code>mode="grep"</code> (default, wraps
        <code>grep -rnI</code>) or <code>mode="semantic"</code>
        (dependency-free lexical index). Swap the <code>embed()</code> step
        to use a real embedding model.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="toolDispatch"
      />
      <Diagram
        name="searchBackendSelection"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>MCP</h2>
      <p>
        Amber connects to MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers over the
        supported transports, extending the agent with external tools and
        services. The <code>ServerManager</code> handles lifecycle.
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="mcpSessionLifecycle"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Sub-agents</h2>
      <p>
        <code>SubAgentExecutor</code> runs focused sub-agents with their own
        context and a custom system prompt. Execution mode is
        runtime-configurable:
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Serial</strong>: sub-agents run one at a time; sequential requests share the system-prompt prefix, keeping provider prompt caches warm.</li>
        <li><strong>Parallel</strong>: sub-agents run concurrently, capped by <code>max()</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <Diagram
        name="subagentExecution"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Compression Pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        Two-phase pipeline: classify-then-extract. Phase 1 classifies the
        conversation into core/context/prune segments. Phase 2 extracts
        memory and skill operations. The pipeline is pure, the context is
        only read. Classify/extract requests are assembled from a working
        copy of the snapshot, so a failed pipeline leaves the context
        untouched by construction. The rebuild (<code>clear()</code> +
        <code>push()</code>) happens only on success.
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>Classify: segments the conversation by importance</li>
        <li>Extract: pulls memory/skill ops from the LLM</li>
        <li>Prune: replaces bulky tool outputs with placeholders</li>
        <li>Headroom: enforces 25% headroom after compression</li>
      </ul>
      <Diagram
        name="compressionPipeline"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Security Model</h2>
      <p>Amber treats model output as untrusted input.</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Workspace confinement</strong>: read/write confine paths to the workspace root (default cwd, override with <code>AMBER_WORKSPACE</code>); absolute paths and <code>../</code> escapes are rejected</li>
        <li><strong>Approval gating</strong>: bash tool requires explicit user approval; denied (fail-safe) if no approver</li>
        <li><strong>Process isolation</strong>: commands run in their own process group</li>
        <li><strong>Output capping</strong>: command output capped at 64 KiB</li>
        <li><strong>Timeout</strong>: default 60s timeout on shell commands</li>
      </ul>
      <Diagram
        name="approvalGate"
      />
      <Diagram
        name="pathConfinement"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Host Clients</h2>
      <p>
        The three clients link <code>libagent_core.a</code> +
        <code>libagent_tools.a</code> and communicate via
        <code>AgentHooks</code>:
      </p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>amber</strong>: interactive ncurses TUI (BitchX-inspired multi-window, sessions, slash commands, jobs, plugin controls, approval dialogs)</li>
        <li><strong>amber-cli</strong>: headless CLI for scripts, CI, automation, scheduled jobs</li>
        <li><strong>amber-bench</strong>: benchmark and KPI harness (scenario loader, oracle scorer, recorder as an <code>AgentHooks</code> observer)</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Build System</h2>
      <p>Standard configure + make. The GNUmakefile auto-runs ./configure to generate Makefile from Makefile.in:</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>make          # build everything (lib + cli + tui)
make test     # run unit tests
make check    # smoketest + build hygiene
make lint     # run clang-tidy
make analyze  # run cppcheck
make clean    # remove .o / .d / binaries
make distclean # also removes the generated Makefile</pre>
      </div>
      <Diagram
        name="buildLayering"
      />
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
